1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,096
Lesson 10.4.

2
00:00:02,476 --> 00:00:04,030
Sparse transformers.

3
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:07,867
A thermodynamic chip
does not have all-to-all

4
00:00:07,867 --> 00:00:11,490
connections: each pbit talks
to a fixed number of neighbors.

5
00:00:11,870 --> 00:00:16,967
If we want a language model to live on
the chip, its layers must also be sparse.

6
00:00:17,347 --> 00:00:22,709
The question is how much is lost, and
the answer is surprising: with the same

7
00:00:22,709 --> 00:00:27,375
connection budget, a wide sparse
layer can beat a narrow dense one.

8
00:00:28,483 --> 00:00:31,975
In a sparse layer, each
output unit connects to

9
00:00:31,975 --> 00:00:35,021
only k inputs, chosen by a fixed pattern.

10
00:00:35,401 --> 00:00:39,054
A dense layer of n units
with d inputs has n

11
00:00:39,054 --> 00:00:42,292
· d connections; the sparse one, n · k.

12
00:00:42,672 --> 00:00:45,449
Since each connection
is a multiply-add, the

13
00:00:45,449 --> 00:00:47,973
number of connections measures the cost.

14
00:00:48,353 --> 00:00:52,177
The pattern can be random,
local or block-structured.

15
00:00:53,283 --> 00:00:56,017
To compare fairly, we
fix the budget: eight

16
00:00:56,017 --> 00:00:58,814
thousand one hundred
ninety-two connections.

17
00:00:59,194 --> 00:01:04,197
With degree sixty-four, that is dense,
one hundred twenty-eight units fit; with

18
00:01:04,197 --> 00:01:09,199
degree sixteen, five hundred twelve; with
degree two, four thousand ninety-six.

19
00:01:09,579 --> 00:01:13,533
We train the readout of each
network on a regression task with

20
00:01:13,533 --> 00:01:16,977
a nonlinear teacher and
measure the error on new data.

21
00:01:18,083 --> 00:01:19,927
The result is U-shaped.

22
00:01:20,307 --> 00:01:24,321
The dense network has a
relative error of 0.115.

23
00:01:24,701 --> 00:01:31,096
Making it sparser and wider, the error
drops to 0.083 with degree sixteen.

24
00:01:31,476 --> 00:01:35,877
With lower degree it rises
again: with two inputs per

25
00:01:35,877 --> 00:01:39,779
unit, 0.109, because each
unit sees too little.

26
00:01:40,159 --> 00:01:41,919
There is an optimal intermediate degree.

27
00:01:43,050 --> 00:01:47,134
The scaling-law literature
finds a similar pattern in large

28
00:01:47,134 --> 00:01:51,495
models: at equal compute, sparsity
helps when training is long,

29
00:01:51,495 --> 00:01:55,233
and the optimal degree depends
on model size and data.

30
00:01:55,613 --> 00:01:59,297
For a TSU the constraint goes the
other way: the degree is fixed

31
00:01:59,297 --> 00:02:02,520
by the chip, for example
sixteen, and design consists of

32
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:05,859
choosing the width and the
pattern to make the most of it.

33
00:02:06,983 --> 00:02:10,643
With fixed degree, how does information
get from one end to the other?

34
00:02:11,023 --> 00:02:11,986
By stacking layers.

35
00:02:12,366 --> 00:02:16,623
With a local pattern, the
receptive field grows linearly with

36
00:02:16,623 --> 00:02:20,253
depth; with a small-world
pattern, which mixes local

37
00:02:20,253 --> 00:02:24,300
connections with a few long
jumps, it grows exponentially.

38
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:27,677
So a few layers of degree sixteen suffice

39
00:02:27,677 --> 00:02:30,528
for every unit to depend on all inputs.

40
00:02:31,650 --> 00:02:35,346
In practice, choose the degree
from the hardware and tune

41
00:02:35,346 --> 00:02:39,106
the width at a fixed budget;
use small-world patterns; and

42
00:02:39,106 --> 00:02:42,153
train with static masks,
with no need to prune.

43
00:02:42,533 --> 00:02:46,485
In lab thirty you will do a
degree sweep on a small model.

44
00:02:47,617 --> 00:02:51,172
Common mistakes: comparing
networks with different budgets.

45
00:02:51,552 --> 00:02:56,904
Assuming sparse matrices are fast
on a GPU, when they often are not.

46
00:02:57,284 --> 00:03:00,711
Using purely local patterns
in shallow networks.

47
00:03:01,091 --> 00:03:05,201
And generalizing the optimal degree
of a small example to large models.

48
00:03:06,317 --> 00:03:10,088
In summary, a sparse layer
connects each unit to k inputs,

49
00:03:10,088 --> 00:03:13,599
and at equal budget it can
be better than a dense one.

50
00:03:13,979 --> 00:03:16,465
In our example the
error follows a U-shaped

51
00:03:16,465 --> 00:03:18,720
curve with a minimum at degree sixteen.

52
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:22,107
With fixed degree, depth
and long jumps ensure

53
00:03:22,107 --> 00:03:25,115
that information mixes
across the whole graph.

54
00:03:26,217 --> 00:03:29,261
To practice: compute the
width with degree eight.

55
00:03:29,641 --> 00:03:32,514
Explain why the error
rises at very low degree.

56
00:03:32,894 --> 00:03:36,404
Estimate how many layers are
needed to cover all inputs.

57
00:03:36,784 --> 00:03:40,823
And explain why sparse
layers can be slow on a GPU.
